14:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1c",null,{"guide":{"date":"August 29, 1862","place":"The Second Manassas battlefield around Groveton, Stony Ridge, the unfinished Manassas Gap Railroad, and the Warrenton Turnpike in Prince William County, Virginia","result":"Tactical stalemate on August 29. Repeated United States assaults temporarily penetrated Jackson’s line but did not hold the unfinished railroad. Longstreet’s wing joined Jackson and deployed across the exposed Federal left-front. The battle remained undecided until the Confederate counterattack on August 30","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"The second day of Second Manassas, Friday, August 29, 1862","note":"Episode 176 covered the August 28 fight at Brawner Farm. This episode follows the attacks on Jackson’s railroad position, Longstreet’s arrival, and the evening action on the Warrenton Turnpike."},{"label":"Battle names","value":"Second Manassas and Second Bull Run","note":"The National Park Service also lists Manassas, Manassas Plains, Groveton, Gainesville, and Brawner’s Farm as associated names for the August 28–30 battle."},{"label":"Pope’s objective","value":"Concentrate against Jackson before he could withdraw or receive support","note":"Pope interpreted Jackson’s movements as an attempted escape and directed converging movements toward Groveton and Gainesville."},{"label":"Jackson’s position","value":"Stony Ridge and the unfinished Manassas Gap Railroad","note":"A. P. Hill held the Confederate left, Alexander Lawton commanded the center in place of the wounded Richard Ewell, and William E. Starke commanded the right in place of the wounded William B. Taliaferro."},{"label":"Railroad terrain","value":"Cuts, fills, embankments, and level stretches formed an irregular defensive line","note":"The railroad grade offered cover in many places, but deep cuts, steep fills, and unfinished gaps prevented Jackson’s infantry from using it as one continuous breastwork."},{"label":"Opening Federal force","value":"About 9,000 men under Franz Sigel in the episode’s account","note":"Sigel advanced west from Henry Hill across a front about two miles wide during the morning."},{"label":"Morning attacks","value":"Carl Schurz and Robert H. Milroy struck Jackson’s left and center","note":"Schurz engaged Maxcy Gregg’s South Carolinians near the rocky ridge. Milroy attacked across the Groveton–Sudley Road toward a gap in the unfinished railroad. Both movements were repulsed."},{"label":"Longstreet arrives","value":"Lee and Longstreet reached Jackson at about 10 a.m.","note":"John Bell Hood’s division led Longstreet’s column into position astride the Warrenton Turnpike. By shortly after noon, most of Longstreet’s wing had deployed on Jackson’s right."},{"label":"Federal fire grazes Lee","value":"A Federal bullet grazed Lee’s cheek during reconnaissance","note":"The episode places the incident while Lee examined the Federal line after the two Confederate wings reunited."},{"label":"Confederate concentration","value":"More than 50,000 men available after the two wings reunited","note":"The episode’s figure describes Lee’s combined battlefield force, not the number simultaneously engaged on August 29."},{"label":"Pope reaches the field","value":"About 1 p.m., still unaware that Longstreet was substantially deployed","note":"The Confederate line then extended nearly three miles: Jackson faced southeast north of the turnpike, while Longstreet faced generally east from Jackson’s right."},{"label":"Federal left","value":"Open ground east of Longstreet’s concealed position","note":"Woodland screened Longstreet’s deployment. Porter and McDowell encountered this force south of the turnpike while trying to act on Pope’s orders."},{"label":"Grover’s attack","value":"About 1,500 men charged at roughly 3 p.m.","note":"Cuvier Grover shifted through woods and struck an incompletely defended gap between Gregg’s and Edward L. Thomas’s brigades. Thomas’s Georgians gave way, but Dorsey Pender’s brigade helped close the breach."},{"label":"Grover’s losses","value":"More than one-third of the brigade in the episode’s account","note":"The National Park Service summary gives about 25 percent. Strength and casualty tabulations vary by source and by the units or time span included."},{"label":"Nagle’s penetration","value":"Three IX Corps regiments crossed the railroad against Lawton’s division","note":"Bradley T. Johnson’s Virginia brigade hit James Nagle’s exposed left. Nelson Taylor arrived too late to support the penetration, and a Confederate pursuit briefly threatened a Federal battery."},{"label":"Federal battery fight","value":"Two guns were lost before Hooker restored the line","note":"The episode describes a Confederate pursuit after Nagle and Taylor withdrew, followed by a Federal rally under Joseph Hooker."},{"label":"A. P. Hill’s ammunition","value":"At least one brigade had exhausted its cartridges late in the day","note":"Henry Kyd Douglas recalled details collecting ammunition from dead and wounded soldiers while Hill warned Jackson that another attack could be difficult to stop."},{"label":"Largest assault of the day","value":"Kearny sent ten regiments forward at about 5 p.m.","note":"Kearny’s division broke through parts of Gregg’s and Thomas’s line. Two regiments from Isaac Stevens’s division also penetrated the Confederate defense."},{"label":"Jackson’s last reserve","value":"Jubal Early’s brigade restored the threatened sector","note":"The Federal penetrations were not reinforced in time, and Jackson’s counterattacks recovered the railroad position before dark."},{"label":"Porter and McDowell","value":"Their southward movement encountered Longstreet instead of Jackson’s open flank","note":"Pope’s orders were contradictory and based on an inaccurate picture of the field. Porter did not make the attack against Jackson that Pope expected."},{"label":"Hatch at dusk","value":"Moved west on the Warrenton Turnpike and collided with Longstreet’s troops","note":"McDowell ordered John P. Hatch’s division toward Groveton. The resulting action ended in darkness without a decisive result."},{"label":"Pope recognizes Longstreet","value":"About 7 p.m.","note":"Even after accepting that Longstreet was present, Pope concluded that Lee was reinforcing Jackson or preparing to withdraw rather than preparing a major offensive."},{"label":"August 29 result","value":"Jackson held; Longstreet deployed; neither side launched a decisive general attack","note":"Federal assaults made several local breaches but failed to coordinate or exploit them. The full battle’s Confederate victory belongs to the August 28–30 result, not to August 29 alone."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"“Return to Bull Run” by John J. Hennessy","note":"The official episode page and podcast book list identify the full title as “Return to Bull Run: The Campaign and Battle of Second Manassas.”"},{"label":"Transcript normalizations","value":"Brawner, Irvin McDowell, Ricketts, Fitz John Porter, Sigel, Heintzelman, Taliaferro, Ewell, Starke, Maxcy Gregg, Nagle, Kearny, and Henry Kyd Douglas","note":"The automated transcript produces forms including “Bronner,” “Urban,” “Bricket,” “Fitch,” “Siegel,” “Heinselman,” “Tolliver,” “Yule,” “Stark,” “Maxie,” “Nagel,” “Carney,” and “Henry Kye Douglas.”"}],"maps":[{"title":"Operational setting: the armies converge at Groveton","caption":"Jackson moved from Manassas Junction to Stony Ridge, Pope concentrated from Manassas Junction and Centreville, and Longstreet approached through Thoroughfare Gap and Gainesville. Routes, locations, and movements are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Library of Congress campaign maps, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep177-operational-context.svg","alt":"Generalized operational map showing Jackson at Groveton, Longstreet approaching from Thoroughfare Gap, and Pope concentrating from Manassas Junction and Centreville."},{"title":"August 29 attacks along the unfinished railroad","caption":"Jackson’s divisions held Stony Ridge north of the Warrenton Turnpike. Sigel, Grover, Nagle, Kearny, and Stevens attacked from the east and southeast, while Longstreet deployed south of Jackson and blocked Porter and McDowell. Hatch moved west on the turnpike near dusk. Bars, arrows, and breakthrough markers show generalized relationships, not exact routes, timing, frontages, or troop geometry. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, official reports, Library of Congress maps, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep177-august-29-positions.svg","alt":"Generalized map of Jackson’s divisions along the unfinished railroad, Longstreet south of the turnpike, and the principal Federal attacks on August 29, 1862."},{"title":"Currier and Ives view of the August 29 battle","caption":"Currier and Ives published this hand-colored lithograph under the title “The second battle of Bull Run, fought Augt. 29th 1862.” It is a contemporary popular print, not a surveyed view or precise record of troop positions.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep177-battle-lithograph.jpg","alt":"Hand-colored Currier and Ives lithograph showing United States and Confederate troops fighting at Second Bull Run."}],"moments":[{"time":"4:40","audioTime":280,"title":"The August 28 fight is summarized","detail":"The hosts identify Brawner Farm as the opening action and begin the account of Friday, August 29."},{"time":"5:32","audioTime":332,"title":"Pope renews the attempt to trap Jackson","detail":"After Jackson leaves Manassas Junction, the Brawner Farm fight reveals his position near the old Bull Run battlefield."},{"time":"6:49","audioTime":409,"title":"Federal columns concentrate around Groveton","detail":"King, Ricketts, Porter, Reynolds, Sigel, IX Corps, and Heintzelman’s III Corps receive movements intended to close on Jackson from several directions."},{"time":"8:06","audioTime":486,"title":"Jackson’s line is placed on Stony Ridge","detail":"A. P. Hill holds the left, Lawton the center, and Starke the right along the unfinished railroad north of the Warrenton Turnpike."},{"time":"8:45","audioTime":525,"title":"The railroad is described as an irregular defense","detail":"Cuts, fills, steep banks, and level gaps explain why Jackson’s infantry did not follow the railroad grade continuously."},{"time":"9:24","audioTime":564,"title":"Sigel begins the morning pressure","detail":"About 9,000 men advance west from Henry Hill; Schurz engages Gregg while Milroy attacks toward a gap in the railroad."},{"time":"10:13","audioTime":613,"title":"Lee and Longstreet reach the battlefield","detail":"Hood’s division begins deploying astride the Warrenton Turnpike on Jackson’s right at about 10 a.m."},{"time":"10:31","audioTime":631,"title":"A bullet grazes Lee","detail":"Lee is struck on the cheek while reconnoitering, then continues organizing the reunited Confederate army."},{"time":"10:54","audioTime":654,"title":"Thomas’s brigade describes a Federal penetration","detail":"Marion H. Fitzpatrick of the 45th Georgia recalls the regiment nearly being surrounded and Branch’s brigade helping drive the attackers back."},{"time":"12:06","audioTime":726,"title":"Nagle’s attack is described from the railroad ditch","detail":"Henry H. Pearson of the 6th New Hampshire recounts finding Confederate troops close to the opposite side of the grade and withdrawing under fire."},{"time":"13:36","audioTime":816,"title":"Pope arrives without an accurate picture of Longstreet","detail":"By about 1 p.m., Pope remains focused on Jackson even though Longstreet is substantially deployed to the south."},{"time":"13:51","audioTime":831,"title":"The Confederate line reaches nearly three miles","detail":"Jackson faces southeast north of the turnpike, and Longstreet faces east from Jackson’s right."},{"time":"14:53","audioTime":893,"title":"Fresh Federal formations replace Sigel’s troops","detail":"Elements of Heintzelman’s III Corps and Reno’s IX Corps prepare new attacks on the unfinished railroad."},{"time":"15:04","audioTime":904,"title":"Grover’s brigade attacks","detail":"At about 3 p.m., five regiments move through woods and charge a vulnerable gap between Gregg and Thomas."},{"time":"15:41","audioTime":941,"title":"Thomas gives way at the railroad","detail":"Grover’s men pass the grade, but Gregg shifts troops and Pender arrives before the Federal breach can be reinforced."},{"time":"16:13","audioTime":973,"title":"Nagle attacks Lawton’s division","detail":"Three IX Corps regiments penetrate the railroad position southwest of Grover’s attack."},{"time":"16:32","audioTime":992,"title":"Bradley Johnson hits Nagle’s flank","detail":"The Virginia brigade forces Nagle back; Taylor arrives too late and is also driven from the immediate front."},{"time":"16:49","audioTime":1009,"title":"Hooker restores the Federal line","detail":"A Federal battery nearly falls during the Confederate pursuit, and two guns are lost before Hooker stops the advance."},{"time":"17:57","audioTime":1077,"title":"A. P. Hill reports an ammunition crisis","detail":"Henry Kyd Douglas recalls a brigade without cartridges and Hill warning Jackson that another attack could be difficult to defeat."},{"time":"19:52","audioTime":1192,"title":"Jackson’s line repulses another attack","detail":"Douglas’s account ends with Hill reporting that the new Federal assault has been driven back."},{"time":"20:09","audioTime":1209,"title":"Kearny’s division begins the largest assault","detail":"Ten regiments attack the battered Confederate left at about 5 p.m., while two regiments from Stevens’s division join the penetration."},{"time":"21:44","audioTime":1304,"title":"Early’s brigade restores Jackson’s position","detail":"Jackson commits his remaining reserve, and the unsupported Federal breakthrough is forced back before night."},{"time":"22:01","audioTime":1321,"title":"Porter’s expected attack does not occur","detail":"Porter receives conflicting orders and encounters Longstreet’s large force on Jackson’s right."},{"time":"22:26","audioTime":1346,"title":"Hatch moves west on the turnpike","detail":"McDowell directs Hatch toward Groveton, where the division collides with Longstreet’s troops in a confused dusk action."},{"time":"22:55","audioTime":1375,"title":"Pope accepts that Longstreet is present","detail":"At about 7 p.m., Pope finally revises his estimate but still interprets Lee’s movements as reinforcement or withdrawal rather than preparation for an attack."},{"time":"23:59","audioTime":1439,"title":"The book recommendation is given","detail":"The hosts recommend John J. Hennessy’s “Return to Bull Run: The Campaign and Battle of Second Manassas.”"}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. John Pope","command":"Army of Virginia","subordinates":"Directed the converging movements and repeated assaults against Jackson; reached the battlefield about 1 p.m."},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Franz Sigel","command":"I Corps, Army of Virginia","subordinates":"Carl Schurz’s division, Robert H. Milroy’s brigade, and other I Corps formations conducted the morning attacks."},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Irvin McDowell","command":"III Corps, Army of Virginia","subordinates":"James B. Ricketts’s and Rufus King’s divisions; John P. Hatch led King’s division toward Groveton during the evening action."},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Fitz John Porter","command":"V Corps, Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Moved with McDowell south of the turnpike, encountered Longstreet, and did not make Pope’s expected attack on Jackson."},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Samuel P. Heintzelman","command":"III Corps, Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Joseph Hooker’s division included Cuvier Grover and Nelson Taylor; Philip Kearny’s division made the largest late-afternoon assault."},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Jesse L. Reno","command":"IX Corps","subordinates":"Isaac I. Stevens’s division and James Nagle’s brigade attacked Jackson’s line during the afternoon."},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. John F. Reynolds","command":"Pennsylvania Reserves division","subordinates":"Operated on the Federal left near the Warrenton Turnpike and was assigned to strike Jackson’s right with Sigel’s command."}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Reunited Longstreet’s wing with Jackson and examined the possibility of attacking Pope’s exposed left."},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","command":"Jackson’s wing","subordinates":"Divisions under A. P. Hill, Alexander Lawton, and William E. Starke defended the unfinished railroad."},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. A. P. Hill","command":"Light Division on Jackson’s left","subordinates":"Episode-focused brigades under Maxcy Gregg, Edward L. Thomas, Dorsey Pender, Lawrence O. Branch, and Charles W. Field."},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Alexander R. Lawton","command":"Division in Jackson’s center","subordinates":"Temporarily commanded Richard S. Ewell’s division after Ewell was wounded at Brawner Farm; Bradley T. Johnson and Jubal Early played important roles in the counterattacks."},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. William E. Starke","command":"Division on Jackson’s right","subordinates":"Temporarily commanded William B. Taliaferro’s division after Taliaferro was wounded at Brawner Farm."},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. James Longstreet","command":"Longstreet’s wing","subordinates":"Deployed south of Jackson astride the Warrenton Turnpike; John Bell Hood’s division led the arrival from Thoroughfare Gap."}]}],"people":[{"name":"John Pope","role":"Army of Virginia commander who concentrated against Jackson without timely recognition of Longstreet’s deployment","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep177-john-pope.jpg"},{"name":"Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","role":"Confederate wing commander whose divisions defended Stony Ridge and the unfinished railroad","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/ep177-stonewall-jackson.jpg"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Army of Northern Virginia commander who reunited Jackson and Longstreet on the battlefield","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"James Longstreet","role":"Confederate wing commander whose arrival blocked Porter and extended Lee’s line south of the turnpike","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"A. P. Hill","role":"Light Division commander responsible for the heavily attacked Confederate left","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Franz Sigel","role":"I Corps commander whose troops opened the principal Federal attacks on August 29","side":"United States"},{"name":"Robert H. Milroy","role":"Federal brigade commander who made repeated morning attacks near the railroad grade","side":"United States"},{"name":"Carl Schurz","role":"Federal division commander who attacked Maxcy Gregg’s position on Jackson’s left","side":"United States"},{"name":"Cuvier Grover","role":"Hooker division brigade commander whose bayonet attack temporarily broke Thomas’s line","side":"United States"},{"name":"James Nagle","role":"IX Corps brigade commander whose regiments crossed the railroad before a flank counterattack","side":"United States"},{"name":"Philip Kearny","role":"Federal division commander who led the largest late-afternoon assault against Jackson’s left","side":"United States"},{"name":"Joseph Hooker","role":"Federal division commander who directed Grover and restored the line after the Nagle and Taylor attacks","side":"United States"},{"name":"Fitz John Porter","role":"V Corps commander whose movement south of the turnpike encountered Longstreet’s wing","side":"United States"},{"name":"Maxcy Gregg","role":"Confederate brigade commander defending the rocky ridge on A. P. Hill’s left","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Edward L. Thomas","role":"Confederate brigade commander whose Georgians were driven from part of the railroad line by Grover","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Dorsey Pender","role":"Confederate brigade commander committed to stop Grover’s penetration","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Bradley T. Johnson","role":"Confederate brigade commander whose Virginians attacked Nagle’s exposed flank","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Jubal Early","role":"Confederate brigade commander whose troops reinforced the threatened railroad position late in the day","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"Col. James Cantwell","side":"United States","role":"Commander, 82nd Ohio Infantry, Milroy’s brigade","outcome":"Killed while leading his regiment in the morning attacks against Jackson’s railroad position."},{"name":"Capt. David Gibson","side":"United States","role":"Commander, Company H, 3rd West Virginia Infantry, Milroy’s brigade","outcome":"Killed by a bullet to the forehead while leading a charge toward the railroad embankment. Theodore F. Lang’s postwar account supplies the episode’s opening description."},{"name":"Sgt. Henry Ruffner Paine","side":"Confederacy","role":"First Rockbridge Artillery","outcome":"Mortally wounded by solid shot during a close artillery exchange and died about two hours later. Edward A. Moore’s memoir supplies the episode’s opening account; the transcript renders the surname as “Payne.”"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep177-operational-context.svg","alt":"Generalized map showing the roads and movements that brought Jackson, Longstreet, and Pope together near Groveton.","caption":"Jackson waited near Groveton while Longstreet approached from the west and Pope concentrated from the east and southeast. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Library of Congress maps, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep177-august-29-positions.svg","alt":"Generalized troop-position map of the unfinished railroad fighting on August 29, 1862.","caption":"Federal assaults made temporary breaches in Jackson’s line. Longstreet’s arrival extended the Confederate position south of the turnpike and blocked Porter. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, official reports, Library of Congress maps, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep177-battle-lithograph.jpg","alt":"Currier and Ives lithograph of the Second Battle of Bull Run on August 29, 1862.","caption":"This 1862 popular print presents a stylized view of the fighting. It is useful as a contemporary visual artifact, not as a tactical map.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep177-john-pope.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era glass-negative portrait of United States major general John Pope in uniform.","caption":"John Pope reached the field about 1 p.m. and continued directing attacks against Jackson before recognizing Longstreet’s presence.","source":"Brady’s National Photographic Portrait Galleries, Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep177-stonewall-jackson.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era glass-negative portrait of Confederate major general Thomas J. Stonewall Jackson.","caption":"Jackson’s three divisions defended Stony Ridge and the unfinished railroad throughout August 29.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 177 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2017/01/01/civil-war-podcast-episode-177/"},{"label":"Manassas National Battlefield Park: Second Manassas","url":"https://www.nps.gov/mana/learn/historyculture/second-manassas.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Constant Attack, August 29","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/constant-attack.htm"},{"label":"Manassas National Battlefield Park: Federal Breakthrough interpretive panel","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/information-panel-federal-breakthrough.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service battle summary: Manassas II","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va026"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Battle of Second Manassas staff ride guide","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-D114-PURL-gpo111054/pdf/GOVPUB-D114-PURL-gpo111054.pdf"},{"label":"Official Records of the Union and Confederate Armies, Series I, Volume XII, Part II","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/official-records/016"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Robert Knox Sneden map of the Second Bull Run battlefield","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00241/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Currier and Ives lithograph of the August 29 battle","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/91721281/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: John Pope portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666476/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Stonewall Jackson portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018670324/"},{"label":"Primary account: Theodore F. Lang, “Loyal West Virginia from 1861 to 1865”","url":"https://archive.org/details/loyalwestvirgini00lang"},{"label":"Primary account: Edward A. Moore, “The Story of a Cannoneer under Stonewall Jackson”","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyofconnoneer00moor_0"},{"label":"Official podcast book-recommendations list","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"}]}}]}]
